Section 4 — Explore by Business Challenge
<badge color="success">Core new feature</badge>
4.1 Objective
This section is the most important functional enhancement in the new Insights page.
Its purpose is to allow visitors to discover relevant knowledge by identifying the business challenges they are facing.
Instead of asking visitors:
"What topic would you like to read about?"
The section should help them answer:
"What business challenge are you trying to solve?"
The section must be powered dynamically by the RevOS Business Challenge-Driven Content Discovery System.
4.2 Section heading and introduction
Use the following suggested heading:
Explore by Business Challenge
Supporting description:
"Every revenue organization faces different challenges. Explore practical insights and evidence-driven perspectives based on the problems you are working to solve."
4.3 Dynamic data architecture
Retrieve Business Challenges from the existing Supabase Business Challenge system.
Use the existing database relationships:
•	business_challenges
•	content_business_challenges
•	Existing content table.
Adapt to the actual implemented schema.
The public page must dynamically retrieve:
1.	Active Business Challenges.
2.	Their associated published Insight articles.
3.	The article's primary or secondary relationship to each challenge.
4.	The challenge name, description, and display order.
5.	Article metadata needed to render the article cards.
Do not maintain a separate hardcoded list of Business Challenges in the frontend.
Do not manually configure which article belongs to which challenge.
The database relationship must be the single source of truth.
4.4 Dynamic group generation
The section must generate Business Challenge groups automatically.
For example, suppose the database contains the following Business Challenges:
•	Low Lead Quality
•	Ineffective Lead Qualification
•	Low Pipeline Conversion
•	Poor Pipeline Visibility
•	Inaccurate Sales Forecasting
The page should dynamically create a corresponding group for each eligible challenge.
The groups must be generated from the actual database records, not from a predefined frontend array.
4.5 Article association logic
For each Business Challenge:
1.	Retrieve the Insight articles associated with that challenge.
2.	Include only published Insight articles.
3.	Exclude draft, unpublished, deleted, or otherwise ineligible content.
4.	Exclude inactive Business Challenges from public presentation.
5.	Use the challenge's display order to determine group order.
6.	Use an appropriate article ordering rule within each group, preferably publication date descending unless the existing CMS supports a featured or editorial ordering field.
An article can appear in multiple Business Challenge groups if it addresses multiple challenges.
Do not create duplicate article records.
Each displayed article should link to its existing canonical Insight detail URL.
4.6 Dynamic visibility rules
Implement these rules:
Database condition	Public Insights page behavior
Active challenge with published articles	Display the challenge group
Active challenge without published articles	Hide the group
Inactive challenge	Hide the group
Challenge with only draft articles	Hide the group
Challenge with only unpublished articles	Hide the group
Challenge with articles that have been deleted	Do not display deleted articles
New active challenge with a published associated article	Automatically display a new group
Existing challenge receives another published article	Automatically include the new article
The public page should never display empty Business Challenge groups.
4.7 Business Challenge group design
Each group should contain:
•	Business Challenge name.
•	Short Business Challenge description.
•	Relevant Insight article cards.
•	A link to explore more articles addressing that challenge.
Example conceptual layout:
Low Pipeline Conversion
"Explore the factors affecting pipeline progression and understand how to improve conversion across your sales process."
Article cards:
•	Why Sales Opportunities Fail to Convert
•	How to Diagnose Pipeline Conversion Problems
•	Improving Sales Execution Through Evidence-Based Analysis
Explore All Insights →
Another example:
Poor Pipeline Visibility
"Understand why revenue teams struggle to gain an accurate view of pipeline health, deal progression, and potential revenue outcomes."
Article cards:
•	Building a More Reliable Pipeline Assessment Process
•	Understanding the Root Causes of Pipeline Blind Spots
•	How to Improve Pipeline Visibility and Forecast Confidence
The article titles above are illustrative only. Do not create these articles or display fabricated content.
Use only actual published articles retrieved from Supabase.
4.8 Primary and secondary Business Challenge relationships
The database supports a primary challenge and secondary challenges for each article.
Use this information intelligently.
An article should be discoverable under every Business Challenge associated with it.
However, if the article is shown under its primary challenge, the UI may optionally identify it as a particularly relevant article for that challenge.
Do not unnecessarily repeat the primary/secondary label on every article card.
The primary challenge relationship should be available for future ranking, recommendation, and discovery logic.
4.9 Article card presentation
Use a compact editorial card layout.
Each card may display:
•	Featured image or thumbnail, if available.
•	Article category.
•	Article title.
•	Short excerpt.
•	Publication date.
•	Reading time, if available.
•	Read Insight link.
Avoid making the cards excessively tall.
A three-column desktop layout or a horizontally scrollable card row may be considered depending on the amount of content.
For mobile, use a readable single-column or controlled horizontal-scroll layout.
4.10 Group navigation and interaction
The section may contain several Business Challenge groups.
Design the navigation so visitors can easily browse them without creating a very long and overwhelming page.
Preferred interaction:
•	Display Business Challenge groups in a clear vertical sequence.
•	Each group has a compact title and description.
•	Display a limited number of articles initially.
•	Provide an Explore All link for additional articles.
•	Optionally use a horizontal challenge navigation or category selector to jump to a specific challenge group.
Do not use excessive carousels or complicated animations.
The challenge group must remain understandable and usable without JavaScript-dependent animation.
4.11 Challenge detail page compatibility
Although separate Business Challenge detail pages are outside the scope of this implementation, design the group links and data structure to support future routes such as:
/insights/business-challenges/low-pipeline-conversion/
Future detail pages may include:
•	Business Challenge description.
•	Common symptoms and root causes.
•	Relevant Insight articles.
•	Related Business Challenges.
•	Relevant RevOS engines and capabilities.
•	Practical approaches and recommendations.
Do not implement these new routes now unless they are already part of the application.
For this phase, Explore All can link to an existing filtered listing route or an appropriately implemented challenge-filtered Insights view.
Do not create links to routes that do not exist.
